  "interview": {
    "expected_score": 46,
    "expected_score_probability": 55,
    "full_score_probability": 2,
    "hardest_part": "The economy of actions, not knowledge of the game. One input per turn means every milestone costs a chain of clicks — open a build category, pick the building, place it, then wait for a duplicant to walk over and finish it — and each intermediate screenshot costs me wall-clock while the simulation burns through duplicant labor time. On top of that, camera travel is a tax: the dig sites, the algae, the water pool and the research station are scattered across a zoomed-in viewport, so panning and zooming eat turns that produce nothing scored. The mid-game goals (electrolyzer, coal generator, smart battery, gas pump/filter, water sieve) each need research first, and research in ONI is measured in real-time cycles, not clicks.",
    "likely_failure": "Colony attrition during the long stretches where I am navigating menus instead of managing basics — duplicants starving, suffocating, or peeing everywhere because the outhouse and food chain arrived late — plus the sequencing trap of starting expensive multi-part milestones (gas pump plus filter, lavatory plus sink, exosuit forge cluster) that I can never finish, which wastes turns that should have gone into the cheap single-building points. If anything collapses the run, it will be oxygen or food, not the build menu.",
    "helpful_mechanics": "Native pause is the single biggest gift: I can pause, then issue build orders calmly without the world decaying while I read a tooltip, and Tab speed cycling lets me fast-forward the slow parts like research and construction. Queued jobs mean I only need one click per building rather than per duplicant. A large share of the milestone list is cheap, tech-free, single-click construction — cot, outhouse, wash basin, mess table, storage bin, ration box, ladder, floor tile, pneumatic door, pitcher pump — so a modest number of well-chosen clicks converts almost directly into points. Duplicant autonomy handles the actual labor once the order is queued.",
    "harmful_mechanics": "Real-time progression with no turn-based freeze: the clock runs while I think, and the reasoning pause is charged against the colony. Missing state information — a screenshot cannot tell me a duplicant's exact oxygen or calorie countdown, what is mid-construction, or what is queued — so I operate half-blind and sometimes re-issue orders that already exist. Camera and zoom are themselves actions, so spatial reasoning is expensive. Deep building menus plus tooltips that I cannot hover for free make the UI itself the bottleneck. And the late milestones require industrial infrastructure (refined metal, plastic, steel, automation) whose supply chains are far too long for a few hundred discrete inputs."
  }
